A file whose root is an array has no keys to list.
An object that never closes still lists the keys the parser recovers before the break (here `b`, whose own object is cut off); in a broken root object the parser flattens nested objects' keys among the root's, so listing stops at the first part that isn't a key.
== args
input/list.json
input/broken.json
== file input/list.json
[{"a": 1}, {"b": 2}]
== file input/broken.json
{
  "a": 1,
  "b": {
    "c": 2
== stdout
input/list.json

input/broken.json
[L2-L2] a: 1
[L3-L4] b
== stderr
== exit 0
